A real, machine-level constraint discovered during Phase 2 work, not a code
or test result - recorded here because it genuinely changes what can be done
safely in the remaining phases without the user's input.

This machine's C: drive is a 238 GB volume at roughly 96-98% used
throughout this phase, independent of anything this work did. One
ordinary debug build of the new Tauri desktop shell
(desktop/src-tauri, `cargo build`, no optimisation, no bundling) alone used
1.9 GB, taking free space from ~8.3 GB down to ~6.6 GB. That build cache was
removed immediately after being proven to succeed once (it is exactly as
disposable as Go's own gitignored build/ directory - nothing of value was
lost), which brought free space back up to ~11 GB.

Why this matters for the phases still ahead:

- A Tauri *release* build (optimised, bundled into an MSI/NSIS installer)
  is larger than a debug build, not smaller.
- Setting up a second Linux environment capable of building this same
  desktop app for Linux (webkit2gtk-dev, a full Rust + Node toolchain, on a
  glibc distro - the existing WSL2 lab distro is a minimal, musl-based
  Alpine image purpose-built for the network lab, not a GUI toolchain host)
  would plausibly consume several more gigabytes on top of what is already
  a near-full disk.
- Phase 6 (local AI) requires downloading an actual model file to evaluate
  it - Qwen3-4B at Q4_K_M quantisation is roughly 2.5 GB by itself, and a
  real evaluation per the plan's own §6.4 compares multiple
  candidates/quantisations, which multiplies that.

None of this needs a password, a signing key, or a business/legal decision -
it is a plain resource constraint, and it was not treated as a reason to
stop working. What it does mean: further large, disk-consuming steps
(a Linux GUI toolchain, a full release/installer build, downloading AI model
weights) were not attempted at this stage without first surfacing this,
since doing so on a drive already this full risks a much worse problem than
anything this work is trying to solve. Lower-disk-footprint work
continued in the meantime (Go-side packages, tests, documentation, the
desktop app's Demo-mode frontend, which needed no large downloads beyond the
one-time Rust/Node toolchain install already accounted for above).

Recommendation, stated once and acted on either way: freeing
disk space (or pointing future large downloads/builds at a different drive,
if one exists on this machine) before the work continues into
Phase 4's Linux packaging validation, the Windows installer build, or Phase
6's model evaluation would remove this constraint entirely. No other action
is required to keep the project moving - the phases that do not need large
downloads or a second GUI toolchain continue regardless.
